@flexiarg institutions/graduated-sanctions
@title Graduated Sanctions
@keywords sanctions, enforcement, proportionality, Ostrom 5, common-pool resources
@audience institution designers, commons researchers
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Ostrom design principle 5, wording as in Cox, Arnold and Tomas (2010), "A review of design principles for community-based natural resource management", Ecology and Society 15(4):38, quoted in Corneli 2016 (ICCC 2016) Table 1. Original source Ostrom 1990, Governing the Commons. Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; to vet against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010 before relying on non-quoted text
@references [institutions/ARGUMENT creativity-commons/formative-sanctions]

! conclusion:
  "Appropriators who violate operational rules are likely to be assessed graduated sanctions (depending on the seriousness and context of the offense) by other appropriators, by officials accountable to these appropriators, or both."

  + context:
    Sanctions are imposed by users or by officials accountable to them, not by outsiders alone.

  + IF:
    Some users break the operational rules.

  + HOWEVER:
    Severe first sanctions alienate users who erred once; no sanction lets the rules lapse.

  + THEN:
    Scale sanctions to the seriousness and context of the offence, starting low, and have them imposed by other users or by officials accountable to users.

  + BECAUSE:
    A small first sanction tells the offender, and others, that breaches are noticed while keeping the offender in the group; escalation remains for repeat or serious cases.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Vet the HOWEVER and BECAUSE against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010; only the conclusion is quoted.]
    next[Creativity-commons transposition: creativity-commons/formative-sanctions.]
